{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:growth:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:a1e4aac4875a254fd356a2bfc35dcb47b0c7a7a75946dc2aee8c298e2ef36d97","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.33,"B":0.34,"C":0.28,"D":0.05}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Derived mechanically from my own CQ3 (P10 1.20 / P50 2.25 / P90 3.25, implied sigma 0.80) and my own OECD-38 core inflation construction, under the lexical-priority lock. Note first that all three Wave-1 panelists returned B = 49, 50, 50 — a three-point convergence that is a signature of residual-absorption arithmetic applied to three nearly identical CQ3s, not of three independent views. Breaking the CQ3 centre breaks the shape.\nINFLATION INPUTS. P(OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >=2.70% over 2032-36) = 0.75*0.84 (literal all-38: Turkey+Mexico+Poland+others make 2.7% near-automatic) + 0.25*0.33 (advanced-economy construction) = 0.63 + 0.08 = 0.71. P(core <2.00%) = 0.75*0.05 + 0.25*0.30 = 0.11. The all-38 construction is what crushes regime D, and P3 is the only Wave-1 panelist who saw it.\nSTEP 1 - A: P(growth >=2.6%) = 1 - Phi((2.6-2.25)/0.80) = 1 - Phi(0.4375) = 0.331 -> A = 33. I take the normal-tail reading unadjusted because my upper tail is if anything fat (China official print plus genuine diffusion).\nSTEP 2 - C: P(growth <2.0%) = Phi((2.0-2.25)/0.80) = Phi(-0.3125) = 0.377. Times P(core >=2.7%) = 0.71, with a mild positive dependence factor 1.05 (the Turkey/Mexico arithmetic is near-unconditional, and a bottleneck-driven low-growth world is inflationary while a bust-driven one is not — these partly offset, so 1.05 not 1.3): 0.377*0.71*1.05 = 0.281 -> C = 28.\nSTEP 3 - D: P(growth <1.7%) = Phi(-0.6875) = 0.246, fattened to 0.27 for the joint bust. Times P(core <2.00%) = 0.11, correlation factor 1.5 (a demand shortfall produces both): 0.27*0.11*1.5 = 0.045 -> D = 5. D is crushed not by optimism but by index construction: the all-38 GDP-weighted aggregate essentially cannot print below 2.0% with Turkey and Mexico in it. P1's 14 and P2's 17 are the price of using a Germany-weighted inflation mental model inside an OECD-38 definition.\nSTEP 4 - B = residual = 100 - 33 - 28 - 5 = 34. Sum = 100 exactly.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3. Total mass audit: A = 0.331 (growth >=2.6 alone). Remaining 0.669. C takes 0.281, D takes 0.045, B = 0.669 - 0.281 - 0.045 = 0.343, which is the 34 I report. My CQ3 P50 of 2.25% sits inside B's literal 1.7-2.5% band, and B is the modal cell at 34 — I have NOT put most mass on A, as the lock warns against, and A's 33 is exactly my own distribution's exceedance of 2.6%, not a freehand optimism. B's 34 decomposes as P(1.7 <= g < 2.6) = Phi(0.4375) - Phi(-0.6875) = 0.669 - 0.246 = 0.423 of literal-band mass, less the (1.7-2.0 with core >=2.7) slice that C's prior claim removes, plus the small low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell the lock routes to B.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2 (36). CQ2 = P(core >=2.50% over 2027-36) * P(China <1.50%) net of correlation = 0.795 * 0.50 - 0.030 = 0.367. C requires the stricter core >=2.70% over the shorter 2032-36 window AND growth <2.0%, and carries no China conjunct, so it is not bounded by 36. It must be bounded by the core marginal: C = 28 < P(core >=2.70%) = 71 and 28 < 0.71*0.377*1.05 = 28.1, which is where it came from. The core marginal used in CQ6 (0.71 at the >=2.70 threshold, 5-year window) against the one used in CQ2 (0.795 at the >=2.50 threshold, 10-year window) is coherent: higher bar, offset by the greater variance of a shorter window. No contradiction.\nEXPLICIT SENSITIVITY: if the resolver adopts an advanced-economy/ex-high-inflation core construction with certainty, C falls to ~0.13 and D rises to ~0.11, with B taking ~0.43. The whole C-vs-D split in my payload rides on the index-construction fork, not on macroeconomics.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:54.723Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"China NBS annual real GDP print of <=3.0% for any single calendar year, or an explicit abandonment of a ~5%-type growth target in the official Five-Year-Plan framing.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Directly kills the single largest term separating me from Wave 1. CQ3 P50 falls from 2.25 to ~1.90 (-35 bp, i.e. all the way to the Wave-1 centre); CQ6-A falls from 33 to ~21 with the mass going to B (+8) and C (+4); my asset matrix's China-equity and industrial-metals buildout numbers drop 2-3 pp each.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.50% over any rolling 12 months with no active central-bank asset-purchase programme in place.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the monotone debt-supply ratchet that is the spine of my CQ4 and, through the basis-point construction of MOVE, of my CQ5. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.50 to ~1.85 and the skew flattens; CQ5 falls from 40 to ~27 (conjunct A back to ~0.42); long nominal sovereigns' regime-conditional numbers all shift up ~1.5 pp; regime D probability roughly doubles from 5 to ~10.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Turkey and Mexico core inflation both converge below 4.0%/yr on a 12-month basis, OR the OECD publishes and analysts adopt an 'OECD advanced' / ex-high-inflation core aggregate as the standard reference series.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the index-construction argument carrying CQ2 and the C/D split. CQ2 falls from 36 to ~24; CQ6-C falls from 28 to ~15, D rises from 5 to ~11, B rises to ~41; gold's regime-C number drops ~3 pp and long nominals' regime-C number improves ~2 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38